      "dcterms:title": "Sum rule for the Compton amplitude and implications for the proton–neutron mass difference",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "Gasser1, J.",
        "Leutwyler1, H.",
        "Rusetsky2, A.",
        "a"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "D:20201204",
      "bibo:doi": "10.1140/epjc/s10052-020-08615-2",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://doi.org/10.1140/epjc/s10052-020-08615-2",
      "dcterms:abstract": "The Cottingham formula expresses the leading contribution of the electromagnetic interaction to the proton- neutron mass difference as an integral over the forward Compton amplitude. Since quarks and gluons reggeize, the dispersive representation of this amplitude requires a sub- traction. We assume that the asymptotic behaviour is dom- inated by Reggeon exchange. This leads to a sum rule that expresses the subtraction function in terms of measurable quantities. The evaluation of this sum rule leads to m p−n QED = 0.58 ± 0.16 MeV.",
      "dcterms:source": "library/s10052-020-08615-2/manifest.jsonld",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "library/s10052-020-08615-2"
    }
  ]
